20:50:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a brilliant question, and the answer reveals something deeply counterintuitive about our universe. We can’t find the centre of the universe because **there isn’t one**—at least not in the way we usually think of a centre. Here’s why: ### 1. The Big Bang wasn’t an explosion *in* space. Most people picture the Big Bang as a single point like a firework going off, with all galaxies flying outward into a pre-existing empty space. If that were true, you could trace the paths of galaxies backward and find a central starting point. But that’s not what happened. The Big Bang was an expansion **of space itself**. Space was created everywhere at once and has been stretching ever since. There is no point in the universe you can point to and say, “It started here.” In a very real sense, the Big Bang happened everywhere. ### 2. The expanding balloon analogy. Imagine the universe as the *surface* of a balloon being inflated. Before you blow it up, put a bunch of dots on it with a marker—these represent galaxies. As you inflate the balloon, every dot moves away from every other dot. From the perspective of any single dot, it looks like all the others are rushing away from it. Now, ask yourself: where is the centre of the *surface* of the balloon? It’s not on the surface itself—it’s in the dimension above it, inside the balloon. The surface is all there is; on it, no point is special, no point is the centre. Our universe is similar, but in three dimensions. We live on the three-dimensional “surface” of a four-dimensional expansion. There is no centre that you can reach by traveling through space. ### 3. The Cosmological Principle. On the largest scales, the universe looks the same in all directions (it’s *isotropic*) and the same from every location (it’s *homogeneous*). This is called the Cosmological Principle. If there were a unique centre, the universe wouldn’t look the same from every point—the centre would be a special place. But all our observations confirm that no matter where you are, galaxies recede from you exactly as if you were at the centre. That’s only possible if there is no unique centre. ### 4. But what about the observable universe? You might have heard that we are at the centre of the *observable* universe—and that’s true, but only because we can only see out to a finite distance (about 46 billion light-years in all directions). That sphere is centred on *us* by definition. But an alien on a galaxy billions of light-years away would see their own observable universe, with themselves at the centre. This isn’t a physical centre of the whole universe; it’s just a horizon of light. So, we can’t find the centre because the concept itself doesn’t apply. The universe is expanding uniformly from everywhere, and every observer sees themselves as being at the centre of the expansion—which is one of the most beautiful symmetries in nature.
